  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Colder air will be accompanied by some wet snow and \nrain showers through the weekend with minor snow accumulations \nexpected across the higher terrain.\n\nToday and tonight, primarily light lake-effect and upslope flow-\ninduced precipitation will continue generally south of the Lower \nGreat Lakes as the northwesterly to northerly mean low-level flow \nremains sufficiently-cold/moist. However, precip should undergo an \nuptick in coverage and become somewhat steadier late this morning \nthrough early this evening as a mid/upper level trough axis and \naccompanying area of greater and deeper synoptic moisture in the \nlow/mid-levels advance southeastward across our region, and moist \nisentropic ascent precedes the trough axis aloft, as does the seeder-\nfeeder process over and downwind of the Lower Great Lakes. With \nthermal profiles remaining marginal for rain versus snow (and \nactually warming a little compared to Friday due to some weak WAA at \nthe surface and aloft ahead of the trough axis), the lower \nelevations should see mainly rain through late this afternoon, \nbefore changing back to a rain/wet snow mix late this afternoon/ \nthis evening and especially overnight tonight. Meanwhile the higher \nterrain should experience primarily wet snow, perhaps mixed with \nrain at times, especially late this morning through early evening. \nDaytime highs should reach the mid 30s to lower 40s today and be \nfollowed by overnight lows in the mid 20s to mid 30s tonight.\n\nRidging at the surface and aloft will build gradually eastward \ninto our region on Sunday through Sunday night. Subsidence \naccompanying the ridge will result in gradual lowering of \ninversion heights and a commensurate drying out of the prime \ndendritic snow growth zone aloft. This will result in lingering \nisolated to scattered upslope flow-induced and lake-effect wet \nsnow and/or rain showers south and southeast of the lakes to end \ngradually from west to east Sunday afternoon through evening. \nDry weather is then expected region-wide through Sunday night. \nAdditional snow accumulations through Sunday are expected to be \n1\" or less in the higher terrain. At lower elevations, any snow \naccumulations are expected to be 0.5\" or less. Highs should \nreach mainly the mid 30's to mid 40's late Sunday afternoon and \nbe followed by overnight lows in the mid 20's to mid 30's around\ndaybreak Monday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry weather will return by Monday, with temperatures \ntrending warmer by midweek.\n\nExpansive/strengthening ridging at the surface and aloft will \ncontinue to build from the Great Plains and across the Great \nLakes and Ohio Valley right through the middle of next week. \nThis feature will bring a return to dry weather and should be\naccompanied by mainly clear to partly cloudy sky. A cool air \nmass ahead of the surface high will linger across Western and \nCentral NY on Monday. Temperatures should then rebound nicely \nthrough midweek as the axis of the ridge slides east of our \nlongitude and allows a warmer southerly to southwesterly flow to\ndevelop across our area. It still appears this low-level WAA \nregime will contribute to daytime highs climbing back to above-\nnormal levels by Thursday, when late afternoon highs should \nreach mainly the mid 50's to lower 60's.",
